docs/spec/06-decisions.md:111:- **Sections extend `AgentResponse` rather than arriving as a parallel `BatchResponse` type.** A second response type would duplicate hints, recovery, audience routing, `human_content`, and the whole `to_call_tool_result()` path, and would leave every consumer choosing between two builders on a distinction (one section or several) they often learn only at render time. `items()`/`kv_items()` stay as one-section sugar over the same storage, and a zero- or one-section response renders byte-for-byte as it does today — the existing `insta` snapshots are the test, so multi-section support is a minor bump, not a major one. Widening `PartialSuccess`'s `completed`/`skipped` from `Vec<String>` to index-bearing `OpRef` **is** a major bump, since `completed[2]:` becomes `completed[2]{index,operation}:` and rendered output differs. Taken now because there is no published consumer to break; taken at all because `Vec<String>` cannot express `results[i]` ↔ `ops[i]`, and that correspondence is the whole point of a batch response.
docs/spec/06-decisions.md:427:- **String quoting (§7.2):** a string is quoted exactly when the spec requires it: empty, leading/trailing space or tab, `true`/`false`/`null`, numeric-looking, contains `:` `"` `\` `[` `]` `{` `}` a comma or a control character, or starts with `-` or `#`. Previously only `,` `"` LF CR triggered quotes, so `-x`, `42`, `true` and `a:b` decoded as other types or structure, and backslashes stayed bare. `PartialSuccess`'s `failed[]` reason column is no longer quoted unconditionally.
docs/spec/06-decisions.md:432:- **Root document and whitespace (§5, §12):** a list document is a root object (list entry, `totalCount`, `help`), the empty type name is the quoted empty key `""`, and documents end without a trailing newline: `render_toon`, `list`, `ToonDocument::render`, `empty_state(_with_hints)`, `Pipeline::render`, and every `AgentResponse` text output (TOON and KV paths, so the two end the same way; the JSON `body` likewise). Block helpers meant for concatenation — `render_hints`, `append_hints`, `render_kv`, `render_recovery`, `DomainError::render`, `StatusResponse::render`, `PartialSuccess::render` — keep their trailing newline, and `append_hints` starts its block on a new line when the buffer does not end in one.
docs/spec/06-decisions.md:440:- **Not TOON:** the KV block pads keys for alignment (`key:   value`; §12 requires exactly one space after the colon), does not quote keys or values per §7.2/§7.3, renders floats with fixed decimals and `Missing` as `—`, and ends with a newline. `StatusResponse` and `render_already_done` are KV blocks with the same issues. `DomainError`/`Error::render` is a KV-shaped block whose `message` is unquoted. The `recovery[N]:` block's items (`  tool: suggestedParams: { k: v } — reason`) are not valid list items, and `PartialSuccess` uses it for its help block; its `completed[N]:`/`skipped[N]:` blocks have bare item lines and its summary value contains unquoted commas. Making these TOON means: one space after every colon and §7.2/§7.3 quoting via `escape_value`/`escape_key` (losing KV alignment), canonical numbers and `null` for `Missing`; `completed`/`skipped` as inline arrays; and recovery hints as a tabular array (e.g. `recovery[N]{tool,params,reason}:` with `params` as JSON text) or list-form objects. Left for a maintainer decision, since the alignment and the recovery line format are deliberate readability choices.
docs/spec/06-decisions.md:458:- **Recovery hints:** an array of records `{tool, params, reason}`, rendered as the tabular array `recovery[N]{tool,params,reason}:` (§9.3) with `params` a string holding the compact JSON object (the same writer and text as the JSON companions' `params`, `"{}"` when empty) and `reason` `null` when absent. Every element then has the same keys and only primitive columns, so §9.3 prescribes exactly this table for every input. Considered: `params` as a nested object — §1.4 would then make the form depend on the data (a nested-field-group table `{tool,params{project,type},reason}` when every hint has the same param keys, list-form objects per §10 when params or reason differ or a hint has no params, since empty objects disqualify tabular form), so agents would see three shapes for one block; list-form objects for every input are not an option, as a conforming encoder must use the table when detection succeeds. JSON text in a cell costs `\"` escapes but is unambiguous, typed, and the same convention as `toon::list()`'s nested values (§3 host-type normalization). `DomainError`, `AgentResponse` and `PartialSuccess` all append this one block.
docs/spec/06-decisions.md:461:- **Partial success:** the summary stays one field, quoted because of its commas (`partial_success: "2 completed, 1 failed, 1 skipped"`) — restructuring it into numeric fields would duplicate the array lengths. `completed`/`skipped` are inline primitive arrays (§9.1, the form §1.4 prescribes for strings); `failed` stays the `{operation,reason}` table; recovery hints are the shared `recovery` table instead of a `help[N]:` block, since `help` is the array of strings everywhere else. Empty categories are still omitted.
docs/spec/06-decisions.md:544:- **`#[non_exhaustive]` structs:** `FailedOp`, `PartialSuccess`, `KvItem`, `StatusItem`, `RecoveryHint`, `PipelineStep` and `Pipeline`. Their fields stay public (readable and assignable); struct literals and exhaustive destructuring work only inside michi-core. Each has a constructor: `FailedOp::new(op, reason).recovery(hint)`, `PartialSuccess::new(completed, failed, skipped)` (and `Default`), `KvItem::new(key, value)`, `StatusItem::new(key, value).health(h)`, `RecoveryHint::new(tool)` (existing), `PipelineStep::new(id, name, status)`, `Pipeline::new(id, steps)` (and `Default`). Supersedes AC-020 ("`StatusItem` is not `#[non_exhaustive]`") and the "neither is `#[non_exhaustive]`" note on `Pipeline`/`PipelineStep`; `tests/public_construction.rs` now proves the constructors and `tests/ui-fail/api_hygiene_*.rs` prove the attributes.
docs/spec/06-decisions.md:549:- **NAPI coverage:** `renderPartialSuccess`, `renderPipeline` (status and reason as `StepStatus` labels, validated), `emptyStateWithHints` (throws on a reserved type name like `emptyState`) and `alreadyDone` follow the budgeted conversion pattern of "Capacity limits": 10,000 entries per list, 4 KiB per identifier, 1 MiB per name or reason, 64 MiB per call. There is no `truncateInline`: the `truncate` export already is `truncate_inline`.
